title: The Amnesty Act
subtitle: Forgiveness or forgetting?
tags: event
new-page: true
view-if: year = 1872 and month >= 3
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: amnesty_act_seen = 1
face-image: img/portraits/Benjamin_Butler.jpg

= The Amnesty Act

Congress is considering a bill restoring political rights to most former Confederates, allowing them to once again hold federal and state office. Supporters argue that nearly a decade has passed since the Civil War and that it is time to restore the Union.

Around 150,000 men, most of them <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span>, would regain the right to hold office. Supported by **<span style="color: #7A1F5C;">Radical</span>** representative Benjamin Butler to manage the wave of individual petitions, it aims to stabilize the Union.

The **<span style="color: #7A1F5C;">Radicals</span>** oppose unconditional amnesty, insisting it be paired with new civil rights protections. **<span style="color: #9D2933;">Moderates</span>** argue that adding conditions would doom the bill.

[? if liberal_split = 1 : **<span style="color: #DAA520;">Liberals</span>** have embraced the measure, arguing continued political exclusion only delays national reconciliation. ?]

[? if reconstruction_support < 30 : Northern support for Reconstruction has faded. Public opinion is largely on the bill's side. ?]

[? if radical_strength >= 40 : The **<span style="color: #7A1F5C;">Radicals</span>** have enough influence to make civil rights protections a serious possibility, not just a symbolic gesture. ?]

- @support_clean: Back the bill as written, no conditions.
- @support_conditions: Back amnesty only if paired with civil rights protections.
- @oppose: Come out against the bill.
- @let_it_pass: Stay out of it and let Congress decide.

@support_clean
on-arrival: {!
    Q.amnesty_act = 1;
    Q.amnesty_conditions = 0;

    Q.conservative_dissent -= 5;
    Q.conservative_strength += 3;
    Q.liberal_dissent -= 6;
    Q.liberal_strength += 3;
    Q.moderate_dissent -= 2;
    Q.radical_dissent += 10;
    Q.radical_strength -= 3;

    Q.dem_relation += 6;
    Q.reconstruction_support -= 4;
    Q.freemen_rep -= 5;
    Q.middle_class_rep += 2;
    Q.rural_dem -= 2;

    for (let state of Q.southern_states) {
        Q[state + "_rural_dem"] = Math.min(100, (Q[state + "_rural_dem"] || 0) + 8);
        Q[state + "_middle_class_dem"] = Math.min(100, (Q[state + "_middle_class_dem"] || 0) + 5);
        Q[state + "_rural_rep"] = Math.max(0, (Q[state + "_rural_rep"] || 0) - 5);
    }

    Q.kkk_strength = Math.min(1000, (Q.kkk_strength || 0) + 15);
    Q.whiteL_strength = Math.min(1000, (Q.whiteL_strength || 0) + 10);
!}

We back the bill without conditions. It passes.

The **<span style="color: #9D2933;">Moderates</span>** and **<span style="color: #B5651D;">Conservatives</span>** welcome the decision, while the **<span style="color: #DAA520;">Liberals</span>** call it long overdue.

The **<span style="color: #7A1F5C;">Radicals</span>** denounce the measure. Across the South, freedmen fear the return of political leaders determined to roll back Reconstruction and restore white <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democratic**</span> rule.

@support_conditions
on-arrival: {!
    let supportStrength = (Q.radical_strength || 0) + (Q.moderate_strength || 0);
    let oppositionStrength = (Q.conservative_strength || 0) + (Q.liberal_strength || 0);

    Q.radical_dissent -= 3;
    Q.moderate_dissent -= 2;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 5;
    Q.liberal_dissent += 4;
    Q.dem_relation -= 3;

    if (supportStrength > oppositionStrength) {
        Q.amnesty_act = 1;
        Q.amnesty_conditions = 1;
        Q.freemen_rep += 4;
        Q.reconstruction_support += 2;
        Q.middle_class_rep += 2;
        Q.radical_dissent -= 3;
        Q.dem_relation -= 4;
        Q.amnesty_civil_rights_bonus = 1;

        for (let state of Q.southern_states) {
            Q[state + "_rural_dem"] = Math.min(100, (Q[state + "_rural_dem"] || 0) + 4);
            Q[state + "_raceEqualitySupport"] = Math.min(100, (Q[state + "_raceEqualitySupport"] || 0) + 5);
        }

        Q.kkk_strength = Math.min(1000, (Q.kkk_strength || 0) + 8);

    } else {
        Q.amnesty_act = 1;
        Q.amnesty_conditions = 0;
        Q.freemen_rep -= 3;
        Q.reconstruction_support -= 3;
        Q.radical_dissent += 6;
        Q.moderate_dissent += 3;

        for (let state of Q.southern_states) {
            Q[state + "_rural_dem"] = Math.min(100, (Q[state + "_rural_dem"] || 0) + 7);
            Q[state + "_rural_rep"] = Math.max(0, (Q[state + "_rural_rep"] || 0) - 4);
        }

        Q.kkk_strength = Math.min(1000, (Q.kkk_strength || 0) + 12);
    }
!}
go-to: conditions_result

We support amnesty, but only alongside new civil rights protections. If former Confederates are restored to office, the rights secured since the war must be protected as well.

@conditions_result

[? if amnesty_conditions = 1 : Former Confederates regain their political rights, but the amnesty is paired with new civil rights protections. Neither side is satisfied. **<span style="color: #7A1F5C;">Radicals</span>** wanted stronger guarantees while opponents object to any conditions at all. It is an uneasy compromise, but a compromise nonetheless. ?]

[? if amnesty_conditions = 0 : The civil rights provisions are stripped from the bill before it reaches the floor. **<span style="color: #B5651D;">Conservatives</span>** and **<span style="color: #DAA520;">Liberals</span>** refuse to accept any conditions, and the amnesty passes on its own. The **<span style="color: #7A1F5C;">Radicals</span>** blame us for chasing a compromise that never had the votes. ?]

@oppose
on-arrival: {!
    Q.amnesty_act = 0;

    Q.radical_dissent -= 4;
    Q.radical_strength += 3;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 6;
    Q.liberal_dissent += 7;
    Q.moderate_dissent += 3;
    Q.dem_relation -= 6;

    Q.freemen_rep += 3;
    Q.middle_class_dem += 3;
    Q.rural_dem += 2;
    Q.reconstruction_support += 1;

    Q.kkk_militancy = Math.min(1.5, (Q.kkk_militancy || 0.1) + 0.06);
    Q.whiteL_militancy = Math.min(1.5, (Q.whiteL_militancy || 0.35) + 0.04);

    if (Q.liberal_split === 0) {
        Q.liberal_dissent += 5;
    }
!}

We oppose the bill. A decade is not enough. The men who led secession and now support violent resistance to Reconstruction have done little to earn back political power.

The **<span style="color: #7A1F5C;">Radicals</span>** approve and freedmen leaders welcome our stand.

**<span style="color: #DAA520;">Liberals</span>** condemn the decision while **<span style="color: #B5651D;">Conservatives</span>** grow increasingly frustrated. Across the South, paramilitary groups become more defiant.

@let_it_pass
on-arrival: {!
    Q.amnesty_act = 1;
    Q.amnesty_conditions = 0;

    Q.moderate_dissent -= 1;
    Q.radical_dissent += 5;
    Q.liberal_dissent -= 3;
    Q.conservative_dissent -= 2;
    Q.dem_relation += 3;
    Q.reconstruction_support -= 2;
    Q.freemen_rep -= 2;

    for (let state of Q.southern_states) {
        Q[state + "_rural_dem"] = Math.min(100, (Q[state + "_rural_dem"] || 0) + 6);
        Q[state + "_rural_rep"] = Math.max(0, (Q[state + "_rural_rep"] || 0) - 3);
    }

    Q.kkk_strength = Math.min(1000, (Q.kkk_strength || 0) + 10);
!}

We stay silent and let Congress decide.

The bill passes with little difficulty. Our silence avoids a fight with Congress, but the outcome is no different than if we had endorsed it.

Former Confederates regain political rights across the South. Freedmen leaders looked to Washington for leadership and found none.